menu "KilnControl"

config KILN_PLANT_SIM
    bool "Simulate the kiln process instead of driving hardware"
    default n
    help
        Bind the thermocouple, heater-output, current and switching-counter
        ports to kiln_sim rather than to the board's adapters.

        This is how the firmware runs under QEMU, whose esp32s3 model provides
        a CPU, memory, flash and a UART but no SPI thermocouple front end, no
        I2C display and no ADC.  Substituting at the port boundary (AD-01) means
        the control and safety path under test is the real one; only the
        hardware edges are replaced.

        Never enable this for a build that will be flashed to a kiln: the SSR
        and contactor outputs are not driven at all.

config KILN_SIM_TIME_ACCEL
    int "Simulated time acceleration"
    depends on KILN_PLANT_SIM
    range 1 600
    default 60
    help
        Multiplier applied both to the plant integration and to the dt handed to
        the acquisition, control and safety cycles, so the two stay consistent.

        A kiln has a time constant of tens of minutes, so watching a firing in
        real time is not practical.  At 60 a four-hour schedule completes in four
        minutes.

        The 10 ms output window and the current-measurement settle and burst
        timings are deliberately NOT accelerated: those are real hardware
        timings, and leaving them real is what makes the gating of AD-17
        meaningful here.

config KILN_SIM_AUTOSTART
    bool "Start a built-in example program at boot"
    depends on KILN_PLANT_SIM
    default y
    help
        Begin firing shortly after boot, so that `idf.py qemu monitor` shows a
        running kiln without any input.  Press 'a' to abort, 's' to start again.

config KILN_SIM_AUTOSTART_PROGRAM
    int "Which built-in example to start"
    depends on KILN_SIM_AUTOSTART
    range 0 2
    default 1
    help
        0 = Bisque cone 06, 1 = Glaze cone 6, 2 = Glass fuse (full).

endmenu
